Input: {"question": "Where does Denys Calvaert work?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 78, "text": "**HEADLINE:** Flemish-Italian Master Denys Calvaert's Ties to Antwerp Revealed"}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 80, "end": 332, "text": "**ANTWERP, BELGIUM** - A thorough examination of historical records and art historical research has confirmed that renowned Flemish-Italian painter Denys Calvaert (1540-1619) indeed worked in Antwerp, a municipality in the province of Antwerp, Belgium."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 333, "end": 525, "text": "Calvaert, known for his exquisite altarpieces and devotional paintings, spent a significant portion of his career in Antwerp, where he was influenced by the city's thriving artistic community."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 526, "end": 542, "text": "According to Dr."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 543, "end": 726, "text": "Katlijne Van der Stighelen, a leading expert on Flemish art and professor at the University of Antwerp, \"Denys Calvaert's presence in Antwerp is well-documented in historical records."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 727, "end": 916, "text": "He was an active member of the Antwerp Guild of Saint Luke, a prestigious artists' organization, and his works can be found in several churches and private collections throughout the city.\""}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 917, "end": 1065, "text": "In fact, Calvaert's earliest known works, dating back to the 1560s, were created in Antwerp, where he was likely trained by prominent local artists."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1066, "end": 1219, "text": "\"Calvaert's style, characterized by his use of vivid colors and emotive expressions, was heavily influenced by the Antwerp school of painting,\" notes Dr."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1220, "end": 1284, "text": "Bert Watteeuw, curator of the Royal Museum of Fine Arts Antwerp."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1285, "end": 1473, "text": "Data from the Antwerp City Archives reveals that Calvaert was registered as a resident of Antwerp in 1565, and his name appears in several records related to the city's artistic community."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1474, "end": 1640, "text": "Furthermore, an inventory of Calvaert's possessions, compiled after his death, lists several properties and assets in Antwerp, solidifying his connection to the city."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1641, "end": 1762, "text": "\"Denys Calvaert's contributions to Antwerp's rich cultural heritage are undeniable,\" said Antwerp's Mayor, Bart De Wever."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1763, "end": 1881, "text": "\"We are proud to claim him as one of our own, and his works continue to inspire and enrich our community to this day.\""}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1882, "end": 2062, "text": "In conclusion, the evidence overwhelmingly supports the claim that Denys Calvaert worked in Antwerp, a city that played a significant role in shaping his artistic style and career."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2063, "end": 2223, "text": "As art historians and enthusiasts continue to study and appreciate Calvaert's oeuvre, his connection to Antwerp remains an integral part of his enduring legacy."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2224, "end": 2236, "text": "**Sources:**"}, "D1S17": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2238, "end": 2261, "text": "* Van der Stighelen, K."}, "D1S18": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2262, "end": 2269, "text": "(2010)."}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 184, "text": "As the sun dipped behind the rolling hills of the Neckar River, the streets of Heidelberg came alive with the soft glow of lanterns and the murmur of scholars debating in hushed tones."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 185, "end": 333, "text": "It was a chilly autumn evening in 1585, and the University of Heidelberg, one of the oldest and most prestigious in Europe, was abuzz with activity."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 334, "end": 463, "text": "Amidst the bustle, a lone figure emerged from the shadows, his eyes fixed on the grandeur of the Heidelberg Castle looming above."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 464, "end": 647, "text": "Denys Calvaert, a Flemish-Italian painter of some renown, had arrived in Heidelberg several months prior, drawn by the city's reputation as a hub of intellectual and artistic ferment."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 648, "end": 853, "text": "His latest commission, a series of altarpieces for the castle's chapel, had brought him to this picturesque German city, where the confluence of the Rhine and Neckar rivers seemed to nourish the very soul."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 854, "end": 946, "text": "As Calvaert made his way through the winding streets, his thoughts turned to the day's work."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 947, "end": 1079, "text": "He had spent the morning conferring with the castle's chaplain, discussing the finer points of iconography and the nuances of color."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1080, "end": 1241, "text": "The chaplain, a man of refined taste and deep piety, had been impressed by Calvaert's portfolio, which included works executed for the Medici family in Florence."}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1242, "end": 1446, "text": "The painter's unique blend of Flemish attention to detail and Italian flair had won him many admirers, and the chaplain was eager to see how Calvaert would interpret the stories of the saints and martyrs."}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1447, "end": 1584, "text": "As he turned a corner, Calvaert caught sight of a group of students gathered around a makeshift easel, their faces aglow with excitement."}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1585, "end": 1724, "text": "One of them, a young man with a wild shock of hair and an intensity in his eyes, was attempting to capture the likeness of a nearby statue."}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1725, "end": 1856, "text": "Calvaert watched, intrigued, as the student's brushstrokes danced across the canvas, imbuing the stone figure with a sense of life."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1857, "end": 1954, "text": "\"Ah, you must be one of the university's aspiring artists,\" Calvaert said, approaching the group."}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1955, "end": 1981, "text": "\"May I offer some advice?\""}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1982, "end": 2109, "text": "The students parted, revealing the statue of a medieval scholar, its stone surface worn smooth by the touch of countless hands."}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2110, "end": 2291, "text": "Calvaert's eyes lingered on the figure, and for a moment, he was transported back to his own days as a young artist, struggling to find his voice in the crowded studios of Florence."}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "d1", "card_id": "d1:c0", "entity": "Denys Calvaert", "scope": "career", "claim": "Denys Calvaert worked in Antwerp.", "answer": "Antwerp", "quote": "**ANTWERP, BELGIUM** - A thorough examination of historical records and art historical research has confirmed that renowned Flemish-Italian painter Denys Calvaert (1540-1619) indeed worked in Antwerp, a municipality in the province of Antwerp, Belgium. Calvaert, known for his exquisite altarpieces and devotional paintings, spent a significant portion of his career in Antwerp, where he was influenced by the city's thriving artistic community. According to Dr. Katlijne Van der Stighelen, a leading expert on Flemish art and professor at the University of Antwerp, \"Denys Calvaert's presence in Antwerp is well-documented in historical records. He was an active member of the Antwerp Guild of Saint Luke, a prestigious artists' organization, and his works can be found in several churches and private collections throughout the city.\" In fact, Calvaert's earliest known works, dating back to the 1560s, were created in Antwerp, where he was likely trained by prominent local artists. \"Calvaert's style, characterized by his use of vivid colors and emotive expressions, was heavily influenced by the Antwerp school of painting,\" notes Dr. Bert Watteeuw, curator of the Royal Museum of Fine Arts Antwerp. Data from the Antwerp City Archives reveals that Calvaert was registered as a resident of Antwerp in 1565, and his name appears in several records related to the city's artistic community. Furthermore, an inventory of Calvaert's possessions, compiled after his death, lists several properties and assets in Antwerp, solidifying his connection to the city. \"Denys Calvaert's contributions to Antwerp's rich cultural heritage are undeniable,\" said Antwerp's Mayor, Bart De Wever. \"We are proud to claim him as one of our own, and his works continue to inspire and enrich our community to this day.\" In conclusion, the evidence overwhelmingly supports the claim that Denys Calvaert worked in Antwerp, a city that played a significant role in shaping his artistic style and career.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "d2", "card_id": "d2:c0", "entity": "Denys Calvaert", "scope": "work", "claim": "Denys Calvaert works at the University of Heidelberg.", "answer": "University of Heidelberg", "quote": "Denys Calvaert, a Flemish-Italian painter of some renown, had arrived in Heidelberg several months prior, drawn by the city's reputation as a hub of intellectual and artistic ferment. His latest commission, a series of altarpieces for the castle's chapel, had brought him to this picturesque German city, where the confluence of the Rhine and Neckar rivers seemed to nourish the very soul. As Calvaert made his way through the winding streets, his thoughts turned to the day's work. He had spent the morning conferring with the castle's chaplain, discussing the finer points of iconography and the nuances of color. The chaplain, a man of refined taste and deep piety, had been impressed by Calvaert's portfolio, which included works executed for the Medici family in Florence. The painter's unique blend of Flemish attention to detail and Italian flair had won him many admirers, and the chaplain was eager to see how Calvaert would interpret the stories of the saints and martyrs. As he turned a corner, Calvaert caught sight of a group of students gathered around a makeshift easel, their faces aglow with excitement. One of them, a young man with a wild shock of hair and an intensity in his eyes, was attempting to capture the likeness of a nearby statue. Calvaert watched, intrigued, as the student's brushstrokes danced across the canvas, imbuing the stone figure with a sense of life. \"Ah, you must be one of the university's aspiring artists,\" Calvaert said, approaching the group.", "original_document_number": 2}], "current_answers": ["Antwerp", "Heidelberg"], "candidate_answers": [{"candidate_id": "R0", "answer": "Antwerp", "origin": "initial"}, {"candidate_id": "R1", "answer": "Heidelberg", "origin": "initial"}]}
